export const SELF_SUPERVISED_MODULES = [
  {
    id: 'ssl_overview',
    title: 'Self-supervised Learning Overview',
    subtitle: 'Label bottleneck, pretext tasks, generative vs contrastive vs predictive paradigms',
    difficulty: 'foundational',
    estimatedMin: 35,
    tags: ['ssl', 'pretraining', 'representation', 'pretext tasks', 'foundation models'],
    summary: `Self-supervised learning converts unlabeled data into a supervision signal by designing pretext tasks where the label is derived from the data itself — the key insight is that predictions about the structure of data (next token, masked region, augmentation invariance) force the model to encode semantically rich representations. Unlike supervised learning, the bottleneck is not annotation cost but architecture and pretext task design. SSL representations transfer well because the pretext tasks are diverse enough to capture general structure, while supervised labels are narrow proxies for one specific task. The modern ML stack is now almost entirely SSL-first: foundation models (LLMs, ViT-based vision models, multimodal CLIP-style models) pretrain via SSL on internet-scale data, then adapt cheaply to downstream tasks — making SSL the dominant paradigm for any data-rich domain.`,
    keyPoints: [
      'The label bottleneck: human annotation does not scale to the volume of data available on the internet. ImageNet (1.2M labeled images) required millions of hours of human effort; Common Crawl (petabytes of text) requires zero labeling — SSL exploits this asymmetry.',
      'Pretext tasks as automatic supervision: the supervision signal is constructed from the data structure itself. Examples: predict the next token (GPT), predict the masked token (BERT), predict rotation angle (RotNet), predict relative patch position (Doersch et al. 2015), predict the color of grayscale images (colorization). The model never sees "real" labels during pretraining.',
      'Generative SSL (predict masked/next token): BERT-style masked language modeling, GPT-style autoregressive next-token prediction, MAE for images. The model must reconstruct held-out content — forces learning of syntax, semantics, and local/global structure. Loss is reconstruction loss (cross-entropy for tokens, MSE for pixels).',
      'Discriminative/Contrastive SSL: instead of predicting held-out content, the model learns to distinguish between similar pairs (positives, from the same example) and dissimilar pairs (negatives, different examples). SimCLR, MoCo, CLIP. Loss is contrastive: push positives together in embedding space, push negatives apart.',
      'Predictive SSL without negatives: BYOL, VICReg, Barlow Twins — the model predicts the representation of one view from another without explicitly using negatives. These methods avoid the false negative problem (treating different images of the same class as negatives) but require careful design to prevent representation collapse.',
      'Why SSL representations transfer: pretext tasks impose diversity of prediction targets — a model that predicts masked tokens must encode morphology, syntax, semantics, and world knowledge simultaneously. Supervised labels collapse this to a single taxonomy. The learned features are therefore more general-purpose. Transfer is strongest when pretraining data distribution overlaps with downstream data.',
      'Foundation model paradigm: SSL pretraining on massive data → small fine-tuning or prompting for downstream tasks. The separation between representation learning and task-specific adaptation is now the dominant paradigm at FAANG. GPT-4, Gemini, Claude all follow this pattern for text; DALL-E, Stable Diffusion for images; Wav2Vec for audio.',
      'The "free labels" misconception: SSL is not supervised learning with clever label construction. Pretext task labels (rotation angles, masked tokens) contain entirely different information from semantic labels. The useful signal comes from pretext tasks that correlate with semantic structure — not all pretext tasks do. RotNet rotation prediction helps with object recognition; predicting image noise level does not.',
      'Emergent properties at scale: SSL models trained at scale exhibit emergent capabilities not present at smaller scales — few-shot in-context learning (GPT-3), zero-shot classification (CLIP), compositional reasoning. These properties are not explicitly trained for; they arise from the interaction of scale, diversity of pretext signal, and architecture.',
    ],
    checkQuestions: [
      { q: 'Explain why a model trained with supervised ImageNet labels (1000-class classification) transfers less well than a CLIP model trained on 400M image-text pairs, even though both see similar amounts of compute.', a: 'Supervised ImageNet training collapses the entire diversity of visual information into 1000 class logits — everything not relevant to the 1000 categories is discarded by the gradient signal. CLIP\'s contrastive objective must distinguish among 400M unique text descriptions, forcing the model to encode fine-grained attributes, spatial relationships, style, and semantics that ImageNet categories ignore. When fine-tuning on a new task (e.g., medical imaging, satellite imagery, counting objects), CLIP representations already contain relevant features; ImageNet representations may not. Additionally, CLIP\'s pretraining distribution is vastly broader — web-scraped image-text pairs cover scientific diagrams, artwork, satellite imagery, and microscopy, while ImageNet covers mostly everyday objects.' },
      { q: 'What is a pretext task, and what makes one "good"? Give two examples of good pretext tasks and one bad one.', a: 'A pretext task is a self-supervised prediction problem where the label is derived automatically from the data. A good pretext task: (1) requires understanding high-level semantic structure to solve it, (2) cannot be solved by low-level shortcuts. Good examples: (1) Masked language modeling — predicting masked words requires syntactic parsing and world knowledge; low-level features (character n-grams) are insufficient. (2) Contrastive image augmentation invariance — treating two augmented crops of the same image as positives requires the model to capture object identity rather than color statistics. Bad example: Predicting image file size — compressibility correlates with texture density but not semantic content; solving it requires no meaningful understanding of image content.' },
      { q: 'A team trains an SSL model and claims it outperforms the supervised baseline on linear probe accuracy. The manager asks: does that mean SSL is better for the downstream task? What caveats would you raise?', a: 'Linear probe accuracy measures only linear separability of learned features — it tests whether the representation encodes the downstream task\'s structure in a linearly separable way. This is a necessary but not sufficient condition for downstream usefulness. Caveats: (1) Full fine-tuning often closes the gap between SSL and supervised — the representation quality advantage may not survive task-specific adaptation. (2) Linear probe accuracy for one task may not predict accuracy for a different downstream task — a representation that linearly separates ImageNet classes may not separate medical imaging classes. (3) SSL often uses far more data and compute in pretraining — the comparison must account for total compute budget. (4) Latency and model size may differ. The right evaluation is transfer performance on the actual downstream task with the actual compute and data budget.' },
      { q: 'Why does SSL work better on language than on images, and why did it take longer for SSL to dominate vision?', a: 'Language has a discrete, compositional token space with natural masking boundaries — predicting masked tokens is well-defined, and cross-entropy loss on a vocabulary is a clean objective. Vision operates on continuous pixel values with no natural discrete vocabulary, making masked pixel prediction less informative (predicting pixel intensities is mostly a low-level texture reconstruction task). Vision SSL required additional design choices: patch-level masking (MAE), contrastive learning with augmentation pipelines (SimCLR), or discrete visual tokens (BEiT, DALL-E tokenizer). Additionally, visual data has more structural redundancy (neighboring pixels are highly correlated) — models can solve pixel-level objectives by exploiting local statistics rather than semantic understanding, requiring high masking ratios (MAE uses 75%) to prevent this shortcut.' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'contrastive_loss',
    title: 'Contrastive Loss Functions',
    subtitle: 'NT-Xent, InfoNCE, temperature τ, negative mining, uniformity and alignment',
    difficulty: 'intermediate',
    estimatedMin: 45,
    tags: ['contrastive loss', 'NT-Xent', 'InfoNCE', 'temperature', 'hard negatives', 'uniformity'],
    summary: `Contrastive losses train encoders to map similar inputs close together and dissimilar inputs far apart in embedding space. The critical insight of NT-Xent (and InfoNCE) is framing this as a classification problem: among N-1 negatives, identify the one true positive — this results in a softmax cross-entropy loss that naturally scales with the number of negatives and has a principled information-theoretic interpretation as a lower bound on mutual information. Temperature τ controls the sharpness of the distribution and must be tuned carefully: too low forces the model to perfectly separate every pair (gradients concentrate on hard negatives); too high flattens the distribution and weakens the learning signal entirely.`,
    keyPoints: [
      'NT-Xent (Normalized Temperature-scaled Cross Entropy, SimCLR): for a batch of N examples generating 2N views, the loss for positive pair (i,j) is: L_{i,j} = -log[exp(sim(zᵢ,zⱼ)/τ) / Σ_{k≠i} exp(sim(zᵢ,zₖ)/τ)]. The denominator sums over all 2(N-1) negatives. sim(u,v) = uᵀv/(‖u‖‖v‖) is cosine similarity. Total loss averages over all 2N examples.',
      'InfoNCE (van den Oord et al., 2018): the foundational contrastive loss, derived from mutual information maximization. L = -E[log[f(x,c) / Σ_{x\'} f(x\',c)]] where f(x,c) ∝ p(x|c)/p(x). NT-Xent is a specific instantiation of InfoNCE with cosine similarity and temperature scaling. InfoNCE is a lower bound on I(X;C) — more negatives → tighter bound → better representation.',
      'Temperature τ: the most critical contrastive hyperparameter. Low τ (e.g., 0.05): concentrates attention on hard negatives; gradients are dominated by the most confusing negatives; can cause instability if negatives are too hard early in training. High τ (e.g., 1.0): uniform distribution over all negatives; each negative gets similar gradient signal; slow convergence and weak representations. Optimal τ ≈ 0.07–0.2 in practice. SimCLR uses 0.07.',
      'Why more negatives matter: InfoNCE is a lower bound on mutual information — the bound is tighter with more negatives. With N negatives, the model learns to distinguish the positive from N distractors; with 65536 negatives (MoCo v2), the task is significantly harder and richer. In-batch negatives give O(N) negatives for batch size N at no extra cost — why large batches matter for SimCLR.',
      'False negative problem: a key failure mode of in-batch contrastive learning. If two images in the batch belong to the same semantic class (e.g., two cat images), treating them as negatives sends contradictory gradient signals — the model is penalised for correctly mapping similar images near each other. Debiased contrastive loss (Chuang et al., 2020) corrects for false negatives by estimating and subtracting the contribution of same-class negatives from the denominator.',
      'Hard negative mining: negatives that are close to the anchor in embedding space carry more gradient information than easy negatives (already pushed far apart). Strategies: (1) Mine negatives with highest cosine similarity to anchor. (2) MoCo\'s queue provides naturally hard negatives as the queue ages and the encoder updates. (3) Synthetic hard negatives: interpolate between anchor and random negative in embedding space (MoCHi). Hard negative mining accelerates convergence but risks false negatives.',
      'Uniformity and alignment (Wang & Isola, 2020): two properties that fully characterise good contrastive representations. Alignment: positive pairs should map to nearby points — loss = E‖f(x) - f(x⁺)‖². Uniformity: features should be uniformly distributed on the unit hypersphere — L_uniform = log E[e^{-2‖f(x)-f(y)‖²}] where (x,y) are random pairs. Uniformity prevents collapse (all embeddings mapping to the same point). NT-Xent optimises both simultaneously.',
      'Supervised contrastive loss (Khosla et al., 2020): extends NT-Xent to use ground-truth labels. All examples of the same class are positives; all other classes are negatives. L_{sup} = Σ_{i} 1/|P(i)| Σ_{p∈P(i)} -log[exp(sim(zᵢ,zₚ)/τ) / Σ_{a≠i} exp(sim(zᵢ,zₐ)/τ)]. Outperforms cross-entropy in label-efficient regimes and produces more robust representations.',
      'Practical gotcha — L2 normalisation is mandatory: cosine similarity requires embeddings to lie on the unit sphere. Without L2 normalisation, the loss degenerates — a model can minimise contrastive loss by scaling up all embeddings to make all cosine similarities near 1. SimCLR normalises both the projector output and the target before computing NT-Xent.',
    ],
    checkQuestions: [
      { q: 'SimCLR with a batch size of 256 gives poor results. Increasing to 4096 dramatically improves performance. Explain the mechanism.', a: 'With batch size N, each example has 2(N-1) negatives in the NT-Xent denominator. At N=256, each anchor has 510 negatives — the classification task is relatively easy and the mutual information bound is loose. At N=4096, each anchor has 8190 negatives — the task is much harder, forcing the encoder to learn finer-grained discriminative features. Additionally, the probability of hard negatives (semantically similar images from different classes) increases with batch size — hard negatives provide the strongest gradient signal. MoCo addresses the large-batch requirement by maintaining a queue of negatives, decoupling negative count from batch size.' },
      { q: 'You set temperature τ = 0.01 (very low) and training collapses to NaN loss after 100 steps. What happened?', a: 'At very low τ, exp(sim/τ) becomes numerically extreme — sims near 1.0 result in exp(1.0/0.01) = exp(100) ≈ 2.7×10^43. The softmax denominator overflows to infinity or underflows for non-maximum terms, producing NaN. Even numerically stable implementations struggle: the gradients concentrate entirely on the single hardest negative, leading to explosive gradient updates. The model oscillates or diverges. Fix: (1) Use log-sum-exp stabilisation: subtract max logit before exp. (2) Increase τ to ≥ 0.05. (3) Use gradient clipping. (4) Introduce a learning rate warmup period so embeddings are not yet well-separated when τ-scaled softmax is sharpest.' },
      { q: 'Explain why debiased contrastive loss is needed and sketch how it corrects for false negatives.', a: 'Standard NT-Xent treats all N-1 non-positive examples in the batch as negatives. In a dataset with C classes and batch size N, approximately N/C examples share the same class as the anchor — these are false negatives. The loss penalises the model for mapping co-class images close together, actively contradicting the alignment objective. Debiased contrastive loss (Chuang et al.) estimates the fraction τ of same-class negatives using the ring-loss trick: the true negative distribution p^- can be estimated by subtracting the class-conditional term from the marginal. Corrected denominator: Σ_{neg} exp(sim/τ) ≈ (1/N) Σ_k exp(sim_k/τ) - (τ_+/N) Σ_{pos} exp(sim_pos/τ), where τ_+ is the prior probability of a positive. This up-weights the contribution of true negatives and down-weights false negatives.' },
      { q: 'A colleague claims uniformity is just "spreading embeddings apart" and alignment is "pulling similar things together," so NT-Xent is just doing both simultaneously. What is missing from this picture?', a: 'The colleague is directionally correct but misses the tension and tradeoff between the two objectives. Maximising uniformity pushes all embeddings apart — taken alone, it would map every pair maximally far apart. Maximising alignment pulls positive pairs together — taken alone, it would collapse all embeddings to a single point. NT-Xent balances these by requiring the positive pair to be closer than all negatives. But critically, the optimal solution is not simply "points uniformly on a sphere with positives clustered" — the optimal geometry depends on the number of classes, the augmentation distribution, and the ratio of positive-pair similarity to negative-pair similarity. Uniformity and alignment are diagnostic tools (post-hoc metrics), not the loss itself; NT-Xent can score well on uniformity by pushing one cluster very far from the rest while leaving other clusters collapsed.' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'simclr',
    title: 'SimCLR',
    subtitle: 'Data augmentation, projection head, large-batch contrastive learning, SimCLRv2',
    difficulty: 'intermediate',
    estimatedMin: 45,
    tags: ['SimCLR', 'contrastive learning', 'data augmentation', 'projection head', 'batch size'],
    summary: `SimCLR's central finding is that data augmentation composition is the most important design choice in contrastive learning — the combination of random cropping, color jittering, and Gaussian blur forces the encoder to capture content invariant to photometric and spatial variation. The projection head (a 2-layer MLP appended to the encoder during pretraining) is discarded at fine-tuning time because the representation space immediately before the head is better structured for downstream tasks than the space after: the projection head absorbs the contrastive objective's information loss (invariance to augmentations), freeing the penultimate layer to retain more information. SimCLR's main weakness — quadratic scaling of negatives with batch size — was addressed architecturally by MoCo, not by SimCLR itself.`,
    keyPoints: [
      'Data augmentation pipeline: SimCLR generates two views of each image via random crop + resize, random color jitter (brightness, contrast, saturation, hue), random Gaussian blur, and random horizontal flip. The key finding: random cropping alone gives moderate transfer; adding color jitter nearly doubles the linear probe accuracy; Gaussian blur provides additional gains on ImageNet (less critical on CIFAR). The augmentation composition matters far more than the loss or architecture.',
      'Why cropping works: two random crops of the same image (ResizedCrop to 224×224) may overlap by as little as ~10% of the original area. The model must encode the global object identity despite seeing very different local crops — this forces learning of high-level semantic features rather than texture.',
      'Projection head: a 2-layer MLP (512→2048→128 in original SimCLR) appended to the encoder (ResNet-50). NT-Xent loss is applied to the 128-dim head output. At fine-tuning time, the head is discarded and a linear layer is attached to the 2048-dim encoder output. The head is critical during pretraining (without it, linear probe accuracy drops by ~10%) but always discarded — the contrastive objective imposes invariances in the head space that would destroy useful downstream variation in the encoder space.',
      'Why the projection head is discarded: the contrastive loss pushes representations to be invariant to augmentations. This invariance is useful for the pretraining objective but harmful downstream — for example, color invariance destroys medical imaging features. The head absorbs this "destructive invariance," insulating the encoder representation. The encoder layer thus retains more information about the input than the head output — information that is useful for supervised downstream tasks.',
      'Large batch dependency: SimCLR uses in-batch negatives — all other 2(N-1) examples in the batch are negatives for a given anchor. This requires very large batches (4096–8192) to get enough negatives for a strong contrastive signal. Training with batch 4096 requires 32 TPU cores for 100 epochs. This is prohibitive for most practitioners. MoCo decouples negative count from batch size via a momentum encoder and queue.',
      'What happens without negatives in SimCLR: without negatives, the loss can be minimised by mapping all inputs to the same point (representation collapse). The network trivially satisfies "all positive pairs are identical" by making every representation identical. SimCLR has no mechanism to prevent this without negatives — unlike BYOL which uses stop-gradient and an online/target architecture to implicitly resist collapse.',
      'SimCLRv2 improvements: (1) Deeper projection head: 3-layer MLP instead of 2-layer, with the 2nd layer used at fine-tuning (not 1st as in v1). (2) Larger ResNets (ResNet-152 with 3× width). (3) Knowledge distillation: use the large pretrained model to distill a smaller student via semi-supervised fine-tuning. SimCLRv2 achieves 90.6% top-1 on ImageNet with 1% labeled data, closing most of the gap to supervised training with full labels.',
      'Practical memory constraint: storing all 2N representations in GPU memory simultaneously for loss computation requires 2N × d × 4 bytes. At N=4096, d=128: 2 × 4096 × 128 × 4 = 4 MB — manageable. But the ResNet-50 backbone itself is 25M parameters; gradients + activations for 4096 images at full resolution require ~32 GB VRAM, necessitating multi-GPU or mixed-precision training.',
    ],
    checkQuestions: [
      { q: 'If you remove the projection head and apply the NT-Xent loss directly to the encoder\'s output, what happens and why?', a: 'Linear probe accuracy drops significantly (Chen et al. report ~10% drop on ImageNet). Without the head, the contrastive loss directly pressures the encoder output to be augmentation-invariant. This invariance conflicts with downstream task requirements — for example, color invariance makes the representation discard color information that is useful for distinguishing object classes (red vs. green apple, skin tone, medical scan contrast). The head acts as a buffer: it learns the augmentation-invariant representation that the loss requires, while the encoder output retains richer, more informative features. Think of it as the head absorbing the "destructive compression" while the encoder output serves as a general-purpose representation.' },
      { q: 'A team replicates SimCLR on a proprietary medical image dataset and finds it does not benefit from the color jitter augmentation that is critical for ImageNet. Explain why and what they should do instead.', a: 'ImageNet color jitter matters because color is often spuriously correlated with class identity (green background → outdoor class) — forcing invariance to color makes the model focus on shape and texture, which are more semantically meaningful. In medical imaging (e.g., CT scans, histopathology), color may be diagnostically meaningful (staining intensity, Hounsfield units). Removing color jitter from medical SSL is correct — the augmentation distribution should match the invariances that exist in the domain. Instead, domain-appropriate augmentations include: elastic deformations (common in histology), intensity perturbation within clinically meaningful ranges, random region masking. The general lesson: SimCLR\'s augmentation pipeline was designed for natural images; adapt the augmentation set to match the invariances in your domain.' },
      { q: 'Explain the information bottleneck interpretation of the projection head. What information does each layer encode?', a: 'The encoder backbone (e.g., ResNet-50) encodes rich spatial and semantic features in its final layer — this includes color, texture, shape, spatial layout, and high-level semantics. The projection head MLP compresses this to a 128-dim vector optimised for the contrastive objective: specifically, it learns to be invariant to the augmentation distribution (color, crop, blur). This invariance necessarily discards information that varies across augmentations — color information, exact spatial position, fine-grained texture. The 128-dim projection space is thus an information bottleneck: high augmentation-invariance, low downstream task utility. The encoder\'s 2048-dim penultimate representation retains more information (including augmentation-sensitive features) because it has not yet been through the bottleneck. Discarding the head at fine-tuning time recovers this richer representation.' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'moco',
    title: 'MoCo: Momentum Contrast',
    subtitle: 'Momentum encoder, queue of negatives, MoCo v2/v3, ViT adaptation',
    difficulty: 'intermediate',
    estimatedMin: 50,
    tags: ['MoCo', 'momentum encoder', 'queue', 'contrastive learning', 'ViT', 'BYOL'],
    summary: `MoCo's core insight is that contrastive learning requires a large and consistent dictionary of negatives, but building this from in-batch examples creates a trilemma: large batches require massive compute (SimCLR), memory banks go stale as the encoder updates (Wu et al. 2018), and end-to-end backprop through all negatives is infeasible. MoCo resolves this with a momentum encoder — a slowly-updating copy of the query encoder that produces consistent representations for the queue, with the momentum update (m=0.999) ensuring the queue's key representations are self-consistent even as the query encoder evolves. This was the first practical large-scale contrastive SSL method, enabling competitive performance on a single 8-GPU machine.`,
    keyPoints: [
      'The negative dictionary problem: contrastive learning requires a large, diverse set of negatives per query. Three approaches: (1) In-batch negatives (SimCLR): limited by batch size, requires TPU-scale training. (2) Memory bank (Wu et al.): stores all training examples\' representations, but these go stale as the encoder updates — inconsistent negative keys. (3) Momentum encoder + queue (MoCo): maintains a queue of recent representations from a slowly-evolving encoder — large negative set without requiring large batch or dealing with staleness.',
      'Momentum encoder: the key encoder f_k is not updated by backpropagation. Instead, it follows an exponential moving average of the query encoder f_q: θ_k ← m·θ_k + (1-m)·θ_q, where m=0.999. This ensures the key representations in the queue are produced by nearly-identical encoders — the queue is self-consistent. Backpropagating through the key encoder would require gradient memory for all K negatives in the queue, which is infeasible.',
      'Queue mechanics: a FIFO queue of K negative keys (K=65536 in MoCo v1). After each step: enqueue the current batch\'s keys, dequeue the oldest batch\'s keys. The queue contains representations from the past ~65K/batch_size iterations. This gives far more negatives than any feasible batch while maintaining temporal consistency (most keys were encoded recently).',
      'Why m=0.999? The momentum coefficient controls the trade-off between encoder consistency and adaptation speed. High m (close to 1) → slow encoder evolution → consistent queue but slow learning. Low m → fast adaptation → queue becomes inconsistent (early and late queue entries were encoded with very different parameters). 0.999 gives ~1000 steps of averaging — the queue of 65536 with batch 256 spans ~256 steps, so most keys were encoded with a similar encoder state. Values of 0.9 or below significantly hurt performance.',
      'MoCo v2 (2020): incorporated SimCLR\'s key insights — (1) added the 2-layer MLP projection head (identical to SimCLR), (2) adopted stronger augmentations (Gaussian blur), (3) used cosine learning rate schedule. Result: MoCo v2 matches or surpasses SimCLR v1 with much smaller batch size (256 vs 8192) on a single 8-GPU machine. This demonstrated that SimCLR\'s large-batch requirement was an artifact of in-batch negatives, not fundamental to contrastive SSL.',
      'MoCo v3 (2021): adapts momentum contrast to Vision Transformers (ViTs). Key changes: (1) Both online and momentum networks use ViT backbone. (2) Prediction head added to online network (similar to BYOL). (3) Contrastive loss uses both in-batch negatives and no queue (ViTs are stable without queues at moderate batch sizes). (4) Discovered training instability in ViTs from the patch projection layer — fixed by using a fixed random patch projection (freezing the first layer).',
      'The momentum trick percolates: BYOL uses the same momentum update for its target network. DINO uses it for self-distillation. EMA-based target networks became a standard pattern in SSL after MoCo demonstrated their value. The insight is general: any method that benefits from a consistent target representation (slower to update than the predictor) can use momentum encoder EMA.',
      'MoCo vs SimCLR compute comparison: SimCLR requires batch size 8192 with 128 TPU cores for best results. MoCo achieves comparable performance with batch size 256 on 8 GPUs, with queue size 65536. Total FLOP cost is similar, but MoCo is accessible to any team with a few GPUs — it democratised contrastive SSL. In practice, if you have TPU scale, SimCLR/SimCLRv2 may be simpler to implement; on GPU clusters, MoCo or DINO are preferred.',
    ],
    checkQuestions: [
      { q: 'Why would using m=0.5 instead of m=0.999 in the momentum encoder hurt MoCo\'s performance?', a: 'At m=0.5, the target encoder θ_k updates as θ_k ← 0.5·θ_k + 0.5·θ_q — it adapts to the query encoder with a half-life of ~1 step. This means the queue contains key representations encoded by significantly different encoder states: keys from 256 steps ago were encoded with an encoder that is substantially different from the current target encoder. The contrastive loss treats all queue entries as equivalent negatives, but entries from different encoder states represent an inconsistent distribution — the loss signal becomes noisy because the similarity structure of old keys reflects an outdated representation space. Empirically, m=0.999 provides stable training; m < 0.99 shows significant performance degradation in the original MoCo paper, with m=0.9 losing ~5% linear probe accuracy on ImageNet.' },
      { q: 'MoCo uses a queue (FIFO) rather than a circular buffer with random replacement. Why does ordering matter for the consistency guarantee?', a: 'MoCo\'s consistency guarantee depends on all keys in the queue being encoded by similar encoder states. The key encoder evolves slowly over time — keys encoded T steps ago are from a slightly older encoder than keys from 1 step ago. A FIFO queue ensures that when keys are dequeued, the oldest keys (most stale) are removed first. This minimises the maximum staleness of any key in the queue: at any time, the oldest key is at most K/N_batch steps old. A circular buffer with random replacement could retain very old keys indefinitely — a key from 10,000 steps ago would be encoded by a meaningfully different encoder, injecting inconsistency into the negative distribution. FIFO gives a bounded staleness guarantee: the oldest key is at most K steps behind the current encoder.' },
      { q: 'The MoCo v3 paper found training instability when fine-tuning ViTs with contrastive SSL, traced to the patch embedding layer. Describe the problem and the fix.', a: 'In ViT, the patch embedding is a linear projection that maps 16×16 image patches to embedding vectors (equivalent to a conv layer with stride 16). During contrastive SSL training, this first layer receives gradient signals from all downstream layers through the entire Transformer stack — the gradient signal is highly variable, especially early in training when representations are not yet meaningful. The patch embedding layer oscillates between configurations that produce very different token representations, causing the Transformer\'s attention to be unstable — individual heads collapse to attending to a single token or produce incoherent attention maps, manifesting as training loss spikes. Fix: freeze the patch embedding layer (use a fixed random projection or a sinusoidal projection) for the first several thousand steps, or permanently. This stabilises the token space so the Transformer layers can learn stable attention patterns. MoCo v3 uses a frozen random patch projection, which is sufficient for competitive performance without the instability.' },
      { q: 'Can you use the MoCo queue during fine-tuning for a downstream classification task? Why or why not?', a: 'No — the queue is a pretraining mechanism, not useful at fine-tuning time. During pretraining, the queue maintains a large pool of negatives for the contrastive loss, which has no role in downstream classification. Fine-tuning uses a standard cross-entropy loss with labeled examples; the contrastive objective is dropped entirely. The momentum encoder is also discarded — only the query encoder f_q (with its learned weights) is used as the backbone for fine-tuning, with a classification head attached to the penultimate representation. The projection head (MLP appended during pretraining) is typically also discarded, as in SimCLR. What carries over from pretraining: the query encoder\'s backbone weights. Nothing else from the MoCo infrastructure (queue, momentum encoder, projection head) is used downstream.' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'byol_barlow',
    title: 'Collapse-free SSL Without Negatives',
    subtitle: 'BYOL, Barlow Twins, VICReg — why they don\'t collapse, scale limitations',
    difficulty: 'advanced',
    estimatedMin: 60,
    tags: ['BYOL', 'Barlow Twins', 'VICReg', 'collapse', 'stop-gradient', 'negative-free SSL'],
    summary: `Methods like BYOL, Barlow Twins, and VICReg demonstrate that contrastive negatives are not necessary to learn good representations — but replacing them requires careful mechanisms to prevent trivial collapse where all embeddings converge to a constant. BYOL uses an asymmetric predictor + stop-gradient, and its collapse resistance is now understood to arise from batch normalization introducing implicit gradient variance that acts as implicit negatives. Barlow Twins takes a fundamentally different approach: the loss directly penalises off-diagonal correlations in the cross-correlation matrix between two views, enforcing decorrelation without any negative examples. These methods are elegant and memory-efficient but can underperform contrastive methods at very large scale where the implicit collapse-prevention mechanisms are overwhelmed.`,
    keyPoints: [
      'Why negatives are needed in standard contrastive learning: without explicit negatives, the trivial solution to "make positive pairs similar" is to map all inputs to the same representation (constant embedding). Standard contrastive loss prevents this by requiring the representation to differ from all negatives. Removing negatives requires an alternative mechanism.',
      'BYOL (Bootstrap Your Own Latent, Grill et al. 2020): two networks — online (updated by backprop) and target (momentum EMA of online, m≈0.996). Online has an additional predictor MLP. Loss: minimise L2 distance between predictor(online(view1)) and stop_gradient(target(view2)). No negatives used. The stop-gradient on the target is critical — without it, training collapses.',
      'Why BYOL doesn\'t collapse — the batch norm hypothesis: BYOL uses batch normalisation in both networks. Grill et al. initially could not explain why stop-gradient + EMA prevents collapse. Later analysis (Richemond et al., 2020): BatchNorm implicitly computes statistics across the batch, making each sample\'s representation a function of all other samples in the batch. This batch-level interaction creates an implicit contrastive signal — the network cannot collapse all representations to the same constant because BatchNorm would normalize them to zero-mean, preserving variance. When BatchNorm is replaced with LayerNorm or GroupNorm (which normalize per-sample), BYOL does collapse.',
      'Stop-gradient analysis (Chen & He, 2021): SimSiam removes the momentum encoder entirely — only an online network with predictor and stop-gradient on one branch. The stop-gradient prevents the gradient from flowing back through the target branch, breaking the symmetry that would allow collapse. SimSiam works despite no momentum encoder, confirming stop-gradient is the essential ingredient. The predictor prevents a fixed-point collapse where predictor and target become identical at a degenerate solution.',
      'Barlow Twins (Zbontar et al. 2021): loss = Σᵢ (1 - Cᵢᵢ)² + λ Σᵢ Σⱼ≠ᵢ Cᵢⱼ², where C is the cross-correlation matrix between the two views\' normalised feature vectors: Cᵢⱼ = Σ_b zᵇ_i zᵇ_j / (‖zᵇ_i‖ ‖zᵇ_j‖). The first term (invariance) pushes diagonal elements toward 1 (positive pairs should have correlated features). The second term (redundancy reduction) pushes off-diagonal elements toward 0 (different feature dimensions should not be correlated). No negatives, no stop-gradient, no momentum — just a correlation regulariser.',
      'VICReg (Bardes et al. 2022): decomposes the loss into three terms: (V) variance — each dimension\'s variance across the batch should be ≥ 1 (prevents collapse). (I) invariance — L2 distance between positive pairs should be small. (C) covariance — off-diagonal covariance matrix terms should be zero (like Barlow Twins). VICReg is more interpretable and does not require the two representations to be the same dimensionality (useful for asymmetric architectures).',
      'Scale limitation of negative-free methods: at very large scale (ImageNet-21k, billions of parameters), contrastive methods (MoCo v3, CLIP) consistently outperform negative-free methods. Hypotheses: (1) Implicit collapse prevention via BatchNorm becomes weaker at very large batch sizes where batch statistics stabilise. (2) Without explicit negatives, the loss does not directly penalise representations that are close to each other for semantically different inputs — the redundancy reduction pressure is weaker than explicit negative contrast. (3) Contrastive methods\'s hardest negatives provide a curriculum of increasingly fine-grained discrimination — negative-free methods lack this curriculum.',
      'Practical tradeoffs: BYOL/SimSiam/Barlow Twins do not require large batches (work at batch size 256–512, unlike SimCLR). Simpler implementation (no queue, no large-batch infrastructure). Similar performance to contrastive at ImageNet-1k scale with ResNets. But for ViTs at scale (ViT-B/16 and larger) or with large unlabeled corpora, contrastive and generative methods (MAE) pull ahead.',
    ],
    checkQuestions: [
      { q: 'Remove BatchNorm from BYOL and replace it with LayerNorm throughout. What happens and why?', a: 'Training collapses — all output representations converge to a constant vector. BYOL\'s implicit collapse prevention relies on BatchNorm computing statistics across the batch: the normalisation makes each sample\'s representation a function of all other samples in the batch (each sample is normalised by the batch mean and variance). This creates an implicit dependency between samples: the gradient of sample i\'s loss with respect to the encoder weight is a function of all other samples\' activations through the batch statistics. This implicit cross-sample interaction acts as an implicit contrastive signal — the network cannot collapse all representations to the same value because BatchNorm\'s normalisation would produce zero-variance representations, generating non-zero gradients that drive representations apart. LayerNorm normalises per-sample independently, so no cross-sample interaction exists — the stop-gradient + EMA mechanism alone cannot prevent collapse.' },
      { q: 'Barlow Twins pushes the cross-correlation matrix toward identity. What would happen if you only optimise the invariance term (diagonal → 1) without the redundancy reduction term (off-diagonal → 0)?', a: 'Optimising only the invariance term (diagonal → 1) makes each feature dimension individually correlated between the two views, but allows all feature dimensions to be identical (perfectly correlated with each other). In the limit, the network collapses to a single effective dimension: all 128 (or 2048) feature dimensions encode the same scalar function of the input, just with perfect correlation between views. The redundancy reduction (off-diagonal → 0) term is the anti-collapse mechanism — it forces different feature dimensions to encode different information about the input (feature decorrelation), ensuring the representation uses its full dimensionality. The combination of both terms achieves high-dimensional, diverse representations that are invariant across views.' },
      { q: 'A team trains BYOL on a dataset with very large batch size (65536) and finds it suddenly collapses. They had used batch size 512 successfully before. Explain the mechanism.', a: 'At very large batch sizes, BatchNorm\'s batch statistics (mean and variance) become highly stable and converge to the population statistics — the law of large numbers ensures the batch mean is a reliable estimate of the true mean, and the batch variance is a reliable estimate of the true variance. This stability eliminates the implicit cross-sample interaction that provides BYOL\'s collapse resistance: each sample is now normalised by approximately the same constant (population stats), so the gradient through the batch norm statistics becomes vanishingly small. The implicit contrastive signal disappears. At small batch sizes (512), batch statistics are noisy — the normalisation of sample i genuinely depends on the random composition of the batch, introducing cross-sample gradient signal. Fix: use a smaller batch size for BYOL, use ghost batch norm (compute BN statistics on sub-batches), or switch to a method with explicit collapse prevention at large batch (Barlow Twins, VICReg).' },
      { q: 'When would you prefer Barlow Twins over BYOL for a production pretraining run?', a: 'Prefer Barlow Twins when: (1) You cannot use large batch sizes and want explicit (rather than implicit) collapse prevention — Barlow Twins\' redundancy reduction term is a direct loss, not a BatchNorm artifact. (2) Your architecture uses LayerNorm or GroupNorm (e.g., Transformers) — BYOL\'s collapse resistance does not hold without BatchNorm. (3) You want interpretable loss components: the variance-invariance-covariance decomposition (VICReg) or correlation structure (Barlow Twins) is more debuggable than BYOL\'s implicit dynamics. (4) You need asymmetric architecture (different backbone for the two views) — VICReg (a close relative) does not require matching representation dimensionality. Prefer BYOL when: using CNNs with BatchNorm, targeting ImageNet-scale benchmarks where BYOL\'s empirical results are strong, and you want a simpler implementation (two networks + MSE loss).' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'masked_autoencoders',
    title: 'Masked Autoencoders and Masked Prediction',
    subtitle: 'MAE, BERT masking, high masking ratio, asymmetric encoder-decoder, data2vec',
    difficulty: 'advanced',
    estimatedMin: 60,
    tags: ['MAE', 'masked autoencoder', 'BERT', 'masked prediction', 'ViT', 'data2vec'],
    summary: `Masked autoencoders (MAE) show that generative SSL — reconstructing masked content — can learn representations as powerful as contrastive methods, but the masking ratio must be aggressively high (75% for images) to prevent the model from exploiting local correlations to reconstruct patches without learning global semantic structure. The architectural asymmetry of MAE (lightweight decoder, heavy encoder, mask tokens only in decoder) dramatically reduces training compute relative to contrastive methods that process every patch in both views. The critical conceptual insight is that the reconstruction target should be something the model cannot solve with local statistics alone — patches are redundant, tokens are not, which is why BERT's 15% masking suffices while MAE needs 75%.`,
    keyPoints: [
      'BERT masked language modeling: randomly mask 15% of tokens (replace with [MASK], random token, or keep unchanged at 80/10/10 ratio). Predict the original token at masked positions. 15% masking is sufficient for text because tokens are discrete and semantically dense — predicting a masked word requires syntactic context and world knowledge that cannot be resolved from local character patterns.',
      'Why 15% masking fails for images: image patches are highly spatially redundant — neighboring patches share color statistics, texture, and local structure. A model can reconstruct a 16×16 patch by interpolating from adjacent unmasked patches without learning any semantic information. MAE experiments show that low masking ratios (25%, 40%) produce poor representations; 75% masking gives the best linear probe accuracy because the model cannot rely on local interpolation and must encode global structure.',
      'MAE architecture (He et al. 2022): encoder processes only the unmasked patches (25% of the image) — no [MASK] tokens in the encoder. A lightweight decoder (8 Transformer blocks vs 24 in the encoder) receives the encoded unmasked patches plus learnable [MASK] tokens at the masked positions and reconstructs the original patches. This asymmetry reduces training compute by ~3× relative to processing all patches in the encoder.',
      'Reconstruction target: MAE predicts normalized pixel values in each masked 16×16 patch (channel-wise normalization per patch). This is simpler than contrastive target (embeddings) and more informative than global pixel reconstruction. Not predicting JPEG features or HOG descriptors — raw normalized pixels. Despite being a "low-level" target, the representation learned by the encoder is high-level because the decoder is deliberately weak (it cannot reconstruct details using local information).',
      'iGPT (Image GPT, Chen et al. 2020): autoregressive pixel prediction for images — treat the image as a sequence of downsampled pixel clusters (color palette), apply GPT-style next-token prediction. Competitive but slow: O(n²) attention over pixel sequences. Requires aggressive downscaling (32×32 or 64×64) for tractable training. Superseded by MAE for representation quality, but established that generative approaches work for vision.',
      'Masked image modeling vs contrastive — tradeoffs: MAE (generative): (1) Simpler implementation (no augmentation pipeline, no negative mining, no momentum encoder). (2) Works with any architecture (no constraint to map to a sphere). (3) Learns fine-grained local features (good for detection, segmentation). (4) Requires more fine-tuning steps to match contrastive on classification. Contrastive (SimCLR/MoCo): (1) Faster convergence to good linear probe accuracy. (2) Better zero-shot transfer. (3) Requires careful augmentation design. General pattern: MAE better for dense prediction tasks; contrastive better for classification and retrieval.',
      'data2vec (Baevski et al. 2022): a unified framework across modalities (speech, vision, text). Key idea: instead of predicting raw pixels/waveform samples/tokens, predict the latent representation of the full (unmasked) input as produced by an exponential moving average (EMA) teacher. This combines the generative SSL structure (encoder-decoder, masking) with the representation target of predictive SSL (BYOL-style teacher). Avoids the issue that pixel/waveform reconstruction encourages low-level features — predicting teacher latents encourages high-level semantic features. data2vec v2 adds masked image modeling-specific improvements for speed.',
      'BEiT (BERT Pre-Training of Image Transformers): predict discrete visual tokens (from a DALL-E dVAE tokenizer) at masked positions, rather than continuous pixel values. More like BERT\'s token prediction — uses a categorical cross-entropy loss instead of MSE. The discrete tokenizer is pretrained separately; BEiT uses it as a frozen vocabulary for MIM prediction. Result: better linear probe accuracy than pixel-level MAE, but requires the additional tokenizer pretraining step. Successor: BEiT v2, BEiT v3 extend to multimodal settings.',
    ],
    checkQuestions: [
      { q: 'MAE uses 75% masking. If you reduce it to 25%, what happens to the representation quality and why?', a: 'Representation quality degrades significantly. With 25% masking, the encoder processes 75% of the image patches and the decoder has abundant local context to reconstruct the 25% masked patches by interpolation from neighbors. The decoder can solve the reconstruction task using local texture statistics and color continuity without requiring the encoder to learn any global semantic structure. The encoder is therefore not pressured to encode object identity, scene context, or high-level semantics — it only needs to encode local features sufficient to reconstruct nearby patches. MAE\'s experiments show a smooth monotonic improvement in ImageNet linear probe accuracy as masking ratio increases from 0% to 75%, with the optimum at 75%. Beyond 75%, the task becomes so hard that reconstruction quality degrades without further representation improvement. The 75% threshold is where local interpolation becomes insufficient and the encoder must capture global structure.' },
      { q: 'Why is the MAE decoder deliberately made shallow and weak, even though a stronger decoder would produce better reconstruction?', a: 'The MAE decoder is intentionally weak (8 Transformer blocks vs 24 encoder blocks, smaller embedding dimension) because stronger decoders would shift the reconstruction burden from the encoder to the decoder. A strong decoder with a powerful attention mechanism could reconstruct masked patches by performing a kind of "internal inpainting" using the decoded context, reducing the requirement for the encoder to produce high-quality semantic representations. The weak decoder means any reconstruction quality comes primarily from the encoder\'s representation quality — the encoder must encode enough information about the image\'s global content that the lightweight decoder can recover the masked patches. Additionally, the asymmetric design reduces total compute: encoder processes 25% of patches (the unmasked ones), decoder processes 100% of patches — making the encoder compute dominant and the decoder cheap.' },
      { q: 'Compare MAE and data2vec on the same ViT backbone. For which downstream task would you prefer MAE, and for which would you prefer data2vec?', a: 'MAE predicts normalized pixel values — the reconstruction target is low-level but the representation is general-purpose. data2vec predicts EMA teacher latents — these are high-level semantic representations that the reconstruction pressure is directly aligned with. For dense prediction (object detection, semantic segmentation, depth estimation): MAE tends to be better — predicting pixel values encourages the encoder to retain local spatial detail and fine-grained texture, which is critical for detecting object boundaries and segmenting regions. For linear probing / classification: data2vec tends to be better — predicting semantic teacher latents more directly forces the encoder to organize representations by semantic category. For transfer to other modalities: data2vec has a unified framework (same algorithm for audio, text, vision) making it more practical when training a single model for multiple modalities. In practice (2022–2024), MAE is more widely deployed for vision due to its simplicity and strong detection/segmentation performance (used in Meta\'s SAM).' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'clip_alignment',
    title: 'CLIP and Multimodal Contrastive Alignment',
    subtitle: 'Image-text contrastive pretraining, zero-shot classification, ALIGN, CLIP failure modes',
    difficulty: 'advanced',
    estimatedMin: 65,
    tags: ['CLIP', 'multimodal', 'contrastive', 'zero-shot', 'ALIGN', 'image-text alignment'],
    summary: `CLIP's breakthrough was not the contrastive loss or the scale — it was the realization that image-text pairs from the web constitute a naturally aligned multimodal dataset, and that a contrastive objective over this alignment produces representations that generalize far beyond what domain-specific supervision achieves. The key non-obvious insight: CLIP's representations are not "multimodal" in the sense of fusing modalities — the image and text encoders live in a shared embedding space but are separately applied. Zero-shot classification works because text descriptions of classes ("a photo of a cat") act as soft classifiers in this shared space. However, CLIP's embeddings encode statistical co-occurrence patterns from web data, not true compositional understanding — it fails systematically on spatial relationships, attribute binding, and fine-grained counting.`,
    keyPoints: [
      'CLIP architecture: separate image encoder (ViT or ResNet) and text encoder (Transformer). Both encoders map inputs to vectors in a shared d-dimensional embedding space (d=512 or 768). The two encoders are not connected — no cross-attention, no fusion. During pretraining: minimize InfoNCE loss over matched image-text pairs using all N²-N non-matching pairs in the batch as negatives.',
      'CLIP pretraining loss: for batch of N (image, text) pairs, the NxN similarity matrix S where S_{ij} = cosine_sim(img_i, txt_j). Loss = mean cross-entropy over rows (image→correct text) and columns (text→correct image): L = -1/(2N) [Σᵢ log softmax(S[i,:]/τ)[i] + Σⱼ log softmax(S[:,j]/τ)[j]]. Temperature τ is learned (not fixed), starting at 0.07.',
      'Zero-shot classification: for an image, encode it with the image encoder. For each candidate class (e.g., 1000 ImageNet classes), encode the text template "a photo of a {class}" with the text encoder. Compute cosine similarity of the image embedding with each text embedding. The class with highest similarity is the prediction. No fine-tuning, no labeled examples. Achieves 76.2% top-1 on ImageNet zero-shot — matching a ResNet-50 trained fully supervised.',
      'Prompt engineering for zero-shot: the text template matters significantly. "a photo of a {class}" outperforms just "{class}" by ~3.5% on ImageNet. Ensembling multiple prompts ("a photo of a {class}", "a rendering of a {class}", "a sketch of a {class}") gives further gains by averaging text embeddings over the ensemble. For specialized domains, prompt engineering is critical — medical CLIP using "a radiology scan showing {diagnosis}" vs. "a photo of {diagnosis}" changes performance substantially.',
      'ALIGN (Jia et al. 2021): same architecture and loss as CLIP, but trained on 1.8B noisy image-text pairs from the web (vs CLIP\'s 400M curated WIT pairs). Key finding: scale compensates for noise — a simple noisy-but-large dataset outperforms a clean-but-small one. ALIGN matches and slightly exceeds CLIP despite noisier data because the diversity of 1.8B pairs covers rare concepts, uncommon visual appearances, and fine-grained distinctions that 400M pairs miss.',
      'Why CLIP embeddings are unexpectedly general: CLIP is trained to align any image with its natural language description. Since natural language can describe visual properties at any level of abstraction (color, texture, style, scene, action, emotion, spatial layout), the image encoder must encode all of these to be alignable with the text. Supervised ImageNet training only requires features sufficient to distinguish 1000 categories — texture and background are often sufficient. CLIP requires features that align with open-vocabulary text — it must encode attributes that ImageNet classifiers routinely ignore.',
      'CLIP failure mode — compositionality: CLIP struggles with compositional descriptions. "A red cube on top of a blue sphere" vs "A blue cube on top of a red sphere" receive similar CLIP scores because the model captures "red cube", "blue sphere", and "on top of" as separate bag-of-words-like signals, not as a compositionally bound description. ARO benchmark (Yuksekgonul et al.): CLIP performs near chance on relation, attribute, and order understanding. Fine-tuning on compositional data or using CLIP as a feature extractor for a compositional head is required.',
      'CLIP failure mode — distribution shift: CLIP was trained on web data, which has specific biases (high-quality photos, English captions, typical camera angles). It fails on distribution-shifted inputs: medical images (not in web data), satellite imagery, microscopy, MNIST-style inputs. The embedding space has poor coverage of these distributions. Fine-tuning CLIP on domain-specific data is essential — but catastrophic forgetting of general knowledge is a risk.',
      'CLIP failure mode — fine-grained attributes and counting: CLIP cannot reliably distinguish "a dog wearing a hat" from "a hat wearing a dog" or count objects in a scene. The text encoder learns distributional semantics (hat and dog co-occur in certain contexts) rather than composing attributes onto specific objects. Counting requires binding a quantity to a specific visual entity — CLIP\'s bag-of-features representation does not support this.',
    ],
    checkQuestions: [
      { q: 'Why does CLIP achieve 76% top-1 on ImageNet zero-shot despite never seeing ImageNet labels? What makes this possible?', a: 'CLIP was trained on 400M image-text pairs from the web. Web-crawled images of "golden retrievers", "aircraft carriers", and "strawberries" are extremely common — these concepts appear in millions of image-text pairs. The pretraining distribution covers essentially all 1000 ImageNet classes (and hundreds of thousands of others). The zero-shot classification mechanism — encoding text templates and finding the most similar image embedding — works because the text encoder has learned to map class names to embedding regions that contain the corresponding visual features, and the image encoder has learned to map images to the same regions. The key is that CLIP\'s pretraining included images of these classes paired with text containing the class names, so the joint embedding space aligns visual concepts with their verbal descriptions. It fails (below supervised) on ImageNet because supervised training optimises specifically for 1000 classes; CLIP\'s joint embedding space serves all possible text queries simultaneously, trading specificity for breadth.' },
      { q: 'A team uses CLIP to build a visual similarity search system for a medical image archive. After deployment, they find retrieval quality is poor and the system fails to distinguish between benign and malignant radiological findings. Diagnose the problem and propose fixes.', a: 'Problem diagnosis: CLIP was trained on natural web images with natural language captions. Medical images (CT scans, MRIs, histology slides) are radically out-of-distribution — CLIP\'s image encoder has not seen these during pretraining (or seen very few), so its image embeddings for medical images are not meaningful. The text encoder similarly lacks exposure to medical terminology — "ground glass opacity", "spiculated mass", "pleural effusion" have no distributional meaning in CLIP\'s text embedding space. The embedding space has poor coverage and structure for medical domain. Fixes: (1) Fine-tune CLIP on a large medical image-text dataset (BioViL, PMC-OA, RadBERT-style data) — this adapts both encoders to the medical domain. (2) Use a medical foundation model instead (BioMedCLIP, LLaVA-Med). (3) Train a medical-specific SSL model from scratch (CXR-BERT, CheXpert SSL). (4) Use CLIP as backbone but add domain-specific fine-tuning with carefully curated radiology captions. Monitor: use AUC on retrieval of cases with matching diagnosis, not just semantic similarity.' },
      { q: 'CLIP uses a learned temperature τ instead of a fixed one. Why, and what are the risks of letting τ be learned?', a: 'A fixed temperature requires extensive hyperparameter search per dataset and scale — what works for batch size 32768 on 400M pairs may not work for a smaller experiment. A learned τ allows the model to adapt the sharpness of the contrastive distribution to the difficulty of the current training batch. As training progresses and embeddings become more structured, a lower τ (sharper distribution) may be appropriate to continue providing a training signal on difficult pairs. Risk: τ can collapse to near-zero, making the softmax distribution extremely peaked. If τ → 0, the loss becomes equivalent to forcing every image to be infinitely closer to its matching text than to any other text — this can cause training instability (gradient explosion) when a truly matched pair happens to be embedded far apart early in training. CLIP uses τ = exp(log_τ) with a clamp to prevent τ < 0.01. In practice, τ stabilises around 0.07, similar to SimCLR\'s fixed value — the main benefit is that it is self-calibrating and does not require manual tuning when changing data or architecture scale.' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'ssl_for_tabular',
    title: 'SSL on Non-Image and Non-Text Data',
    subtitle: 'Tabular SSL (SCARF, VIME), graph SSL, audio SSL (wav2vec, HuBERT), domain applicability',
    difficulty: 'intermediate',
    estimatedMin: 50,
    tags: ['tabular SSL', 'SCARF', 'VIME', 'graph SSL', 'wav2vec', 'HuBERT', 'audio SSL'],
    summary: `SSL's dominance in vision and NLP has led practitioners to apply the same principles to tabular, graph, and audio data — with mixed results. The key question in each domain is what constitutes a meaningful augmentation or corruption: for images, random crops and color jitter reflect invariances in natural visual perception; for tabular data, random feature corruption must preserve row-level semantics without violating inter-feature correlations; for graphs, dropping edges is meaningful only if the omitted edges are truly redundant to the node's identity. Audio SSL (wav2vec 2.0, HuBERT) is the clearest success story outside text and images, because speech waveforms have a natural temporal structure that enables powerful masked prediction and contrastive objectives.`,
    keyPoints: [
      'SCARF (Self-supervised Contrastive Learning using Random Feature Corruption): a tabular SSL method. Positive pair: original row + corrupted row (random fraction α of features replaced by random values drawn from that feature\'s marginal distribution). Encoder maps each row to an embedding; NT-Xent loss over positive and in-batch negative pairs. Key design: marginal-distribution corruption is realistic (corruptions look like plausible values, not zeros or noise) and forces the encoder to detect corruption by understanding feature relationships.',
      'VIME (Value Imputation and Mask Estimation): two pretext tasks for tabular SSL. (1) Mask estimation: given a partially corrupted row, predict which features were corrupted (binary mask). (2) Value imputation: reconstruct the original values of corrupted features. Together, these tasks force the encoder to model inter-feature dependencies. Unlike SCARF, VIME uses an explicit reconstruction objective rather than contrastive loss — more interpretable but potentially weaker representations.',
      'When tabular SSL helps: (1) Large unlabeled dataset + small labeled dataset — SSL can provide useful features even with few labeled examples. (2) High-dimensional feature space with many redundant or correlated features — SSL learns to disentangle these. (3) Features with temporal or sequential structure (time-series tabular data) — masking in the time dimension is highly effective. When it doesn\'t help: (1) Small datasets (< 50K rows) — SSL pretraining may not learn meaningful representations. (2) Low-dimensional, handcrafted feature sets — the model has little structure to discover. (3) When XGBoost/LightGBM with feature engineering already captures all relevant structure.',
      'Graph SSL — node-level: predict masked node features (like BERT for nodes, e.g., Hu et al. 2019 "Strategies for Pre-training Graph Neural Networks"). Predict context nodes given center node (graph-level Word2Vec). Graph contrastive methods (GraphCL): augment graphs via random edge drop, feature masking, random walk subgraph — two augmented views are positives, different graphs are negatives.',
      'Graph SSL — graph-level: GraphCL (You et al. 2020) applies augmentations at graph level (drop edges, mask node features, add random subgraphs, attribute masking) and uses NT-Xent over two augmented views of the same graph. Critical finding: the best augmentation is domain-dependent — for molecular graphs, attribute masking works best (molecular identity is defined by atom/bond types); for social networks, edge dropping works best (social connections are structurally defining).',
      'Audio SSL — wav2vec 2.0 (Baevski et al. 2020): CNN encoder produces local audio representations from raw waveform; Transformer contextualizes them; a quantizer (product quantization via Gumbel-softmax) discretizes representations into codebook entries; contrastive loss predicts the quantized representation of masked timesteps. Trained on 960h LibriSpeech + 60K hours unlabeled audio. Fine-tuning on 10 minutes of labeled speech achieves competitive WER — demonstrating extreme label efficiency for ASR.',
      'HuBERT (Hidden-Unit BERT, Hsu et al. 2021): iterative masked prediction for audio. (1) Apply k-means to MFCC features of all training data → offline pseudo-labels. (2) Train a BERT-style masked prediction model to predict pseudo-labels at masked positions. (3) Re-cluster using the learned model\'s representations for better pseudo-labels. (4) Repeat. Unlike wav2vec 2.0, HuBERT avoids online quantization and instead uses offline clustering — more stable training. Achieves state-of-the-art ASR on LibriSpeech, and the representations transfer well to speaker identification, emotion recognition, and speech enhancement.',
      'Key domain lesson: the augmentation/corruption must respect the data\'s semantic invariances. For images, random crops preserve object identity (the object is still in the image). For tabular data, random feature corruption with marginal sampling preserves row identity (the "patient" is still the same patient, just with a few features replaced). If the corruption destroys the semantic identity of the input (e.g., shuffling all features of a tabular row destroys relational structure), the positive pairs no longer represent the same concept and the contrastive objective trains the wrong invariances.',
    ],
    checkQuestions: [
      { q: 'You apply SCARF to a fraud detection dataset with 200 features and find that SSL pretraining does not improve AUC over a baseline XGBoost model trained on the same 5% labeled data. Diagnose possible causes.', a: 'Possible causes: (1) Feature engineering bottleneck: if the 200 features are heavily engineered (ratios, time-delta features, interaction terms), XGBoost can already capture inter-feature relationships through its tree splits. SSL learns to model feature correlations — but those correlations are already encoded in the engineered features. (2) Distribution mismatch: SCARF\'s corruption draws from marginal distributions — if fraud cases have unusual feature value combinations that are rare in the overall distribution (high transaction amount + unusual merchant country + odd time), marginal corruption may not generate useful contrast between fraud and non-fraud regions of feature space. (3) Small unlabeled set: SSL benefits most when unlabeled >> labeled. At 5% labeled with only 5× more unlabeled data, the SSL pretraining signal may be too weak. (4) Sequential/temporal structure not captured: fraud patterns often involve sequences of transactions; SCARF treats each row independently and misses temporal correlations. Better approach: use sequential SSL (masking time-series features, LSTM/Transformer pretraining on transaction sequences).' },
      { q: 'wav2vec 2.0 uses both a quantizer and a contrastive loss. Why is the quantizer needed? What would happen if you used the continuous representations as targets instead?', a: 'The quantizer converts continuous audio representations into discrete codebook entries before using them as contrastive targets. Without quantization: the contrastive loss would use the continuous representations from the CNN encoder (or the Transformer\'s representation of the unmasked context) as targets. This creates a degenerate solution: the model can minimise the contrastive loss by making all representations constant (collapse), or by making the target network simply output the same continuous representation as the query for any input (the query and target networks can lock together). The quantizer breaks this symmetry: discrete codebook assignments are stable and defined independently of the model\'s current continuous representations (or by a Gumbel-softmax approximation). This forces the model to commit to a discrete categorization of audio segments — similar to predicting tokens in BERT rather than predicting arbitrary continuous values. Additionally, discrete targets enable the model to learn a phoneme-like vocabulary (the codebook entries) even without supervision — wav2vec 2.0\'s quantizer learns to cluster audio segments that correspond to similar phonemes.' },
      { q: 'Compare GraphCL\'s edge-drop augmentation for molecular graphs vs social network graphs. Why does the same augmentation have different effects across domains?', a: 'For molecular graphs: edges represent chemical bonds — the bond structure defines the molecule\'s identity and properties (single vs double bond, ring structure, functional groups). Randomly dropping edges changes the molecular graph to a chemically different molecule or an invalid structure. The model trained with edge-drop augmentation learns to be invariant to bond presence — but bond presence IS semantically important for molecular properties. Result: edge-drop augmentation hurts molecular SSL performance. For social networks: edges represent social connections. Two individuals have the same social identity (same person) regardless of whether a few connections are observed or not — in practice, many connections are unobserved due to data collection limitations. Drop-edge augmentation simulates different observed subsets of the same person\'s social network. The model learns to be invariant to specific connection patterns while capturing structural properties (community membership, centrality, role). Result: edge-drop augmentation improves social network SSL performance. The lesson: the correct augmentation reflects what variations leave the semantic identity invariant in that domain.' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'downstream_adaptation',
    title: 'Downstream Adaptation from SSL',
    subtitle: 'Linear probing, fine-tuning, prompt tuning, adapters, catastrophic forgetting, representation evaluation',
    difficulty: 'advanced',
    estimatedMin: 70,
    tags: ['fine-tuning', 'linear probe', 'prompt tuning', 'adapters', 'catastrophic forgetting', 'representation quality'],
    summary: `The practical value of SSL pretraining is realised through downstream adaptation, but the choice of adaptation strategy — linear probing vs full fine-tuning vs parameter-efficient methods — is not purely a compute decision: it reflects a fundamental tradeoff between representation quality and task-specific fit. A common practitioner mistake is to equate high linear probe accuracy with "good SSL" — but linear probeability measures only whether the downstream task's structure is linearly separable in the pretrained space, not whether full fine-tuning will improve further. The highest-quality SSL representations for computer vision (MAE) often have moderate linear probe accuracy but strong fine-tuning performance, precisely because the encoder retains richer, more diverse information that fine-tuning can organise for the downstream task.`,
    keyPoints: [
      'Linear probing: freeze all pretrained weights; train only a linear classification head on labeled downstream data. Reports the linear separability of the pretrained representation with respect to the downstream task. Advantages: fast to evaluate (~epochs), tests representation quality without task-specific adaptation artifacts. Limitations: underestimates fine-tuning performance for high-quality representations; overestimates it for low-quality representations that happen to linearly separate a specific task.',
      'Full fine-tuning: unfreeze all pretrained weights and update them with downstream supervised loss. Maximises downstream task performance. Risk: catastrophic forgetting — the pretrained representations that were useful for other tasks are overwritten during fine-tuning. For SSL models, fine-tuning on small datasets (<10K examples) often causes severe catastrophic forgetting, degrading performance below the linear probe baseline.',
      'Why linear probe and fine-tune rankings diverge: MAE achieves 68% ImageNet linear probe accuracy vs SimCLR v1\'s 69% — they appear similar. But full fine-tuning: MAE achieves 86.9% vs SimCLR\'s ~82%. MAE retains more raw information in its encoder (because it predicts pixels, not embeddings — the encoder must encode local detail) — this extra information is not linearly separable from the ImageNet classes, but is accessible to the non-linear fine-tuned network. The divergence is largest when the downstream task uses features that are present but not linearly organized in the pretrained space.',
      'Catastrophic forgetting: during fine-tuning, gradient updates from the downstream task overwrite weights that encode pretrained knowledge. Most severe when: (1) downstream dataset is small (few labeled examples relative to pretrained parameter count), (2) downstream distribution is very different from pretraining distribution, (3) learning rate is high. Mitigation: (1) Learning rate warmup + low learning rate (1e-5 to 5e-5 for fine-tuning). (2) Layer-wise learning rate decay (LLRD): lower layers get smaller learning rates since they encode general features. (3) Early stopping: fine-tune until val loss starts rising. (4) Elastic weight consolidation (EWC): penalise changes to weights important for the pretrained task.',
      'Layer-wise learning rate decay (LLRD): scale the learning rate by a factor d^{L-l} where d ∈ (0,1) (e.g., 0.75), L is the total number of layers, and l is the current layer index (0=input). Lower layers get very small learning rates (~d^L × base_lr), upper layers get base_lr. This preserves general features in lower layers while allowing task-specific adaptation in upper layers. Used extensively with BERT and ViT fine-tuning — standard practice for NLP fine-tuning.',
      'Prompt tuning: for large language models, add a small set of learnable continuous "prompt" tokens prepended to the input — freeze all model weights and only train the prompt tokens (typically 10–100 tokens × d-dimensional embeddings). Advantages: (1) Extremely parameter-efficient (10–100 prompts vs billions of model parameters). (2) No catastrophic forgetting (model weights unchanged). (3) Separate prompts for different tasks without storing separate model copies. Limitation: underperforms full fine-tuning when the downstream task requires distributional shift from pretraining.',
      'Adapters (Houlsby et al. 2019): insert small 2-layer bottleneck MLPs (h → d_bottleneck → h) after each Transformer layer. During fine-tuning, freeze all pretrained weights and only train adapter weights (~1% of model parameters). Adapters achieve 95% of full fine-tuning performance with 0.5-3% of parameters. LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation): approximate the weight update ΔW = AB (low-rank decomposition, rank r). For a d×d weight matrix: A ∈ ℝ^{d×r}, B ∈ ℝ^{r×d}, r<<d. Only A and B are trained. Merged into the original weight at inference: W_new = W + AB (zero latency overhead). LoRA is now the standard PEFT method for LLMs.',
      'How to evaluate SSL representation quality before fine-tuning: (1) Linear probe accuracy on the downstream task — checks linear separability. (2) k-NN accuracy — check if nearest neighbours in embedding space are semantically similar. (3) Representation isotropy — check whether the embedding space is uniformly distributed (uniformity metric) or has degenerate structure (dimensions with near-zero variance). (4) Probing for specific attributes (color, shape, texture separately) — checks which features are encoded. (5) Transfer to multiple downstream tasks — a representation good for one task but bad for others may have overfit to that pretraining objective.',
      'Practical SSL method selection by downstream task: (1) Dense prediction (detection, segmentation): MAE > contrastive (encodes fine-grained local features). (2) Image classification: DINO, MoCo v3 > MAE for linear probe; comparable at full fine-tuning. (3) Retrieval and zero-shot: CLIP-style multimodal contrastive > anything unimodal. (4) Audio/speech: wav2vec 2.0 or HuBERT (strong label efficiency). (5) Tabular with large unlabeled: SCARF or TabPFN. (6) Low-compute pretraining: BYOL/SimSiam (no large batch). (7) If unlabeled >> labeled (<100 labeled examples): any SSL dominates supervised — the representation quality matters more than adaptation strategy.',
    ],
    checkQuestions: [
      { q: 'A ViT-B/16 model pretrained with MAE achieves 68% linear probe and 86.9% fine-tuned accuracy on ImageNet. A SimCLR model achieves 70% linear probe but only 82% fine-tuned. Your team has 1000 labeled examples. Which model do you choose for adaptation, and with what strategy?', a: 'With 1000 labeled examples, the choice is nuanced. Both models have similar linear probe accuracy (68% vs 70%), so the pretrained features are comparably linearly separable. However, full fine-tuning on 1000 examples carries high catastrophic forgetting risk — the supervised gradient will overwrite pretrained knowledge in 1000 steps. Recommendation: (1) Use MAE if the downstream task benefits from fine-grained local feature detail (e.g., classifying texture-rich classes). MAE retains more raw information, accessible via non-linear fine-tuning. (2) Use SimCLR if the task is classification of high-level categories where linear separability is sufficient, and you will use linear probing or few-shot prompting. (3) Strategy: given only 1000 labels, first evaluate both with linear probe. Then try fine-tuning with aggressive regularization: low learning rate (5e-6), LLRD (layer-wise decay factor 0.75), early stopping with patience 5, and weight decay 0.05. Compare fine-tuning curves — the model that shows stable val loss improvement without overfitting is the better choice. With only 1000 examples, adapters or LoRA are safer than full fine-tuning — they preserve the pretrained weights and prevent catastrophic forgetting.' },
      { q: 'Explain why early stopping is more critical during fine-tuning than during pretraining, and what metric to monitor.', a: 'During pretraining, the loss is typically monotonically decreasing (or noisily decreasing) — there is no overfitting in the traditional sense because the pretraining objective has no held-out test set, and the model is not memorising specific labels. During fine-tuning, two failure modes develop simultaneously: (1) Overfitting: the model memorises the small labeled training set — training loss decreases while validation loss increases. (2) Catastrophic forgetting: the pretrained representations are overwritten by task-specific features — this can cause validation loss to initially decrease (the model is adapting) then increase (general features are being destroyed). Early stopping on validation loss detects overfitting. Monitoring the validation accuracy on the downstream task is the primary metric. For multi-task models, also monitor performance on a held-out set from the pretraining distribution (e.g., ImageNet validation for a model fine-tuned on a medical dataset) — if this accuracy drops sharply, catastrophic forgetting is occurring. The optimal fine-tuning checkpoint is before forgetting begins, which typically coincides with the best downstream validation performance.' },
      { q: 'LoRA and prompt tuning both keep the original model weights frozen. When would you choose one over the other for adapting a large language model to a new domain?', a: 'LoRA modifies the weight updates by learning low-rank decompositions of the weight matrices — the adapted weights are W + AB, which effectively modify the model\'s internal computations while keeping the base weights intact. Prompt tuning adds learnable tokens to the input and trains them, leaving all weights unchanged. Choose LoRA when: (1) The downstream task requires changes to the model\'s internal feature representations — e.g., adapting a general LLM to code generation where the model must learn different syntactic patterns. (2) The downstream distribution differs significantly from pretraining — LoRA provides more capacity to shift the model\'s inductive biases. (3) You have more than ~100 labeled examples — LoRA has more parameters (rank×2×d per layer) and benefits from more training data. Choose prompt tuning when: (1) The downstream task is within the pretraining distribution and can be framed as instruction-following — the model already has the capability, it just needs a task-framing signal. (2) You have very few labeled examples (<50) — prompt tuning has very few parameters (10-100 tokens) and generalises better in extreme low-data regimes. (3) You need to serve many tasks simultaneously from a single model — each task has its own prompt, no separate model copies or weight merging needed. (4) Latency is critical — LoRA weights can be merged into the base model for zero overhead, but requiring identical base weights across tasks prevents this.' },
      { q: 'A team uses k-NN accuracy on a validation set as the primary metric for evaluating SSL representation quality before fine-tuning. What does this metric miss that linear probe catches, and vice versa?', a: 'k-NN accuracy measures whether semantically similar inputs map to nearby regions in the embedding space — it tests local geometry and neighbourhood structure. It does not require linear separability: if two classes form non-linearly separable clusters (e.g., two interleaved spirals in 2D), k-NN will classify correctly but a linear probe will fail. What k-NN misses: it may succeed even if the embedding space has bad global geometry (e.g., all classes collapsed into a small subspace of the full embedding dimension — classes are locally separable but the representation is degenerate). k-NN is also sensitive to the choice of k and the distance metric, and scales poorly with large evaluation sets (O(n×d) per query). Linear probe catches: (1) Whether the representation is globally linearly structured (useful for understanding fine-tuning potential). (2) Whether individual embedding dimensions carry class-discriminative information (vs. all class-discriminative information being in non-linear combinations). (3) Linear probe accuracy is a proxy for how easily a supervised head can learn from the representation — directly relevant to the practical fine-tuning workflow. Together, both metrics are complementary: high k-NN + low linear probe suggests non-linear cluster structure (consider kernel SVMs or non-linear probes); high linear probe + low k-NN suggests global linear structure but poor local neighbourhood geometry (the representation is well-organized globally but individual data points have inconsistent neighbours).' },
    ],
  },
]
